WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:04.476 --> 00:10.236 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:16.516 --> 00:21.076 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:21.586 --> 00:25.586 align:middle
The Federal Open Market
Committee concluded its June

00:25.586 --> 00:27.156 align:middle
meeting earlier today.

00:28.266 --> 00:32.676 align:middle
As was indicated in our policy
statement, the Committee decided

00:32.676 --> 00:36.496 align:middle
to make another modest reduction
in the pace of its purchases

00:36.496 --> 00:38.226 align:middle
of longer-term securities.

00:39.126 --> 00:42.636 align:middle
The Committee maintained its
forward guidance regarding the

00:42.636 --> 00:46.626 align:middle
federal funds rate target
and reaffirmed its view

00:46.626 --> 00:48.696 align:middle
that a highly accommodative
stance

00:48.696 --> 00:51.656 align:middle
of monetary policy
remains appropriate.

00:52.856 --> 00:56.606 align:middle
Today's policy actions reflect
the Committee's assessment

00:57.086 --> 00:58.896 align:middle
that the economy is continuing

00:58.896 --> 01:01.636 align:middle
to make progress
toward our objectives

01:01.636 --> 01:04.246 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

01:05.426 --> 01:08.576 align:middle
In the labor market, conditions
have improved further.

01:09.436 --> 01:14.576 align:middle
The unemployment rate, at 6.3
percent, is four-tenths lower

01:14.576 --> 01:16.486 align:middle
than at the time of
our March meeting,

01:16.486 --> 01:20.796 align:middle
and the broader U-6
measure-which includes

01:20.796 --> 01:24.586 align:middle
marginally attached workers
and those working part time

01:24.586 --> 01:27.686 align:middle
but preferring full-time
work-has fallen

01:27.686 --> 01:28.956 align:middle
by a similar amount.

01:30.066 --> 01:32.296 align:middle
Even given these
declines, however,

01:32.566 --> 01:36.686 align:middle
unemployment remains elevated,
and a broader assessment

01:36.686 --> 01:40.066 align:middle
of indicators suggests
that underutilization

01:40.066 --> 01:42.366 align:middle
in the labor market
remains significant.

01:43.846 --> 01:47.076 align:middle
Although real GDP declined
in the first quarter,

01:47.606 --> 01:50.496 align:middle
this decline appears
to have resulted mainly

01:50.496 --> 01:52.346 align:middle
from transitory factors.

01:53.186 --> 01:56.306 align:middle
Private domestic
final demand-that is,

01:56.306 --> 02:00.706 align:middle
spending by domestic households
and businesses-continued

02:00.706 --> 02:04.426 align:middle
to expand in the first
quarter, and the limited set

02:04.426 --> 02:06.946 align:middle
of indicators of
spending and production

02:06.946 --> 02:09.286 align:middle
in the second quarter
have picked up.

02:10.236 --> 02:13.986 align:middle
The Committee thus believes that
economic activity is rebounding

02:13.986 --> 02:17.516 align:middle
in the current quarter and
will continue to expand

02:17.516 --> 02:19.426 align:middle
at a moderate pace thereafter.

02:20.686 --> 02:22.906 align:middle
Overall, the Committee continues

02:22.936 --> 02:26.766 align:middle
to see sufficient underlying
strength in the economy

02:27.246 --> 02:30.296 align:middle
to support ongoing
improvement in the labor market.

02:31.216 --> 02:33.086 align:middle
Inflation has continued

02:33.086 --> 02:35.856 align:middle
to run below the Committee's
2 percent objective,

02:36.556 --> 02:38.336 align:middle
and the Committee
remains mindful

02:38.336 --> 02:41.736 align:middle
that inflation running
persistently below its objective

02:42.286 --> 02:45.096 align:middle
could pose risks to
economic performance.

02:46.216 --> 02:49.216 align:middle
Given that longer-term
inflation expectations appear

02:49.216 --> 02:53.316 align:middle
to be well anchored, and in
light of the ongoing recovery

02:53.316 --> 02:56.656 align:middle
in the United States and in
many economies around the world,

02:57.406 --> 03:00.086 align:middle
the Committee continues
to expect inflation

03:00.086 --> 03:02.536 align:middle
to move gradually back
toward its objective.

03:03.816 --> 03:07.526 align:middle
The Committee will continue to
assess incoming data carefully

03:08.026 --> 03:10.436 align:middle
to ensure that policy
is consistent

03:10.436 --> 03:14.126 align:middle
with attaining the FOMC's
longer-run objectives

03:14.446 --> 03:17.636 align:middle
of maximum employment and
inflation of 2 percent.

03:19.176 --> 03:20.976 align:middle
This outlook is reflected

03:20.976 --> 03:24.106 align:middle
in the individual economic
projections submitted

03:24.106 --> 03:28.456 align:middle
in conjunction with this meeting
by the FOMC participants.

03:29.386 --> 03:33.476 align:middle
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

03:33.786 --> 03:37.916 align:middle
on his or her own views of
appropriate monetary policy.

03:39.056 --> 03:40.326 align:middle
The central tendency

03:40.326 --> 03:44.336 align:middle
of the unemployment rate
projections is slightly lower

03:44.336 --> 03:48.556 align:middle
than in the March projections
and now stands at 6.0

03:48.556 --> 03:51.906 align:middle
to 6.1 percent at
the end of this year.

03:53.046 --> 03:56.236 align:middle
From there, Committee
participants generally see the

03:56.236 --> 03:58.166 align:middle
unemployment rate declining

03:58.546 --> 04:02.496 align:middle
to its longer-run normal
level by the end of 2016.

04:03.616 --> 04:05.856 align:middle
The central tendency
of the projections

04:05.856 --> 04:12.536 align:middle
for real GDP growth is 2.1
to 2.3 percent for 2014,

04:13.486 --> 04:16.766 align:middle
down notably from the
March projections,

04:17.406 --> 04:20.366 align:middle
largely because of the
unexpected contraction

04:20.366 --> 04:21.436 align:middle
in the first quarter.

04:22.406 --> 04:25.226 align:middle
Over the next two
years, the projections

04:25.226 --> 04:29.276 align:middle
for real GDP growth remain
somewhat above the estimates

04:29.276 --> 04:31.546 align:middle
of longer-run normal growth.

04:32.156 --> 04:35.936 align:middle
Finally, FOMC participants
continue

04:36.026 --> 04:41.106 align:middle
to see inflation moving only
gradually back toward 2 percent

04:41.106 --> 04:44.296 align:middle
over time as the
economy expands.

04:45.446 --> 04:49.956 align:middle
The central tendency of the
inflation projections is 1.5

04:50.036 --> 04:58.586 align:middle
to 1.7 percent in 2014, rising
to 1.6 to 2 percent in 2016.

04:59.816 --> 05:03.426 align:middle
As I noted at the outset,
the Committee decided today

05:03.486 --> 05:05.476 align:middle
to make another measured
reduction

05:05.476 --> 05:07.786 align:middle
in the pace of asset purchases.

05:08.806 --> 05:13.166 align:middle
Starting next month, we will
be purchasing $35 billion

05:13.166 --> 05:17.376 align:middle
of securities per month,
down $10 billion per month

05:17.376 --> 05:18.746 align:middle
from our current rate.

05:19.876 --> 05:23.476 align:middle
Even after today's action
takes effect, we will continue

05:23.476 --> 05:26.436 align:middle
to expand our holdings of
longer-term securities,

05:27.026 --> 05:29.116 align:middle
and we will also
continue to roll

05:29.116 --> 05:31.386 align:middle
over maturing Treasury
securities

05:31.816 --> 05:35.666 align:middle
and reinvest principal payments
from the FOMC's holdings

05:36.016 --> 05:39.886 align:middle
of agency debt and agency
mortgage-backed securities

05:40.116 --> 05:42.356 align:middle
in agency mortgage-backed
securities.

05:42.996 --> 05:44.436 align:middle
These sizable

05:44.436 --> 05:47.456 align:middle
and still-increasing
holdings will continue

05:47.456 --> 05:50.776 align:middle
to put downward pressure on
longer-term interest rates,

05:51.056 --> 05:52.706 align:middle
support mortgage markets,

05:53.046 --> 05:56.016 align:middle
and make financial
conditions more accommodative,

05:56.496 --> 06:00.386 align:middle
helping to support job creation
and a return of inflation

06:00.386 --> 06:01.796 align:middle
to the Committee's objective.

06:03.046 --> 06:05.516 align:middle
Today's announced
reduction in the pace

06:05.516 --> 06:09.626 align:middle
of asset purchases reflects
the Committee's expectation

06:10.136 --> 06:12.776 align:middle
that progress toward
its economic objectives

06:12.776 --> 06:13.686 align:middle
will continue.

06:14.646 --> 06:18.246 align:middle
If incoming information
broadly supports the Committee's

06:18.246 --> 06:22.356 align:middle
expectation of ongoing
improvement in labor markets

06:22.736 --> 06:24.296 align:middle
and inflation moving back

06:24.296 --> 06:26.706 align:middle
over time toward its
longer-run objective,

06:27.486 --> 06:30.876 align:middle
the Committee will likely
continue to reduce the pace

06:30.876 --> 06:34.566 align:middle
of asset purchases in measured
steps at future meetings.

06:35.576 --> 06:39.976 align:middle
However, as I have emphasized
before, purchases are not

06:39.976 --> 06:43.406 align:middle
on a preset course, and
the Committee's decisions

06:43.406 --> 06:47.676 align:middle
about the pace of purchases
remain contingent on its outlook

06:47.736 --> 06:51.786 align:middle
for jobs and inflation
as well as its assessment

06:51.786 --> 06:54.856 align:middle
of the likely efficacy and
costs of such purchases.

06:56.036 --> 06:58.686 align:middle
Let me now turn to the
framework we will be applying

06:58.686 --> 07:00.856 align:middle
as we consider interest
rate policy.

07:02.286 --> 07:05.666 align:middle
In determining how long
to maintain the current 0

07:05.706 --> 07:08.216 align:middle
to one quarter percent
target range

07:08.216 --> 07:09.656 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

07:10.396 --> 07:13.956 align:middle
the Committee will assess
progress-both realized

07:13.956 --> 07:18.116 align:middle
and expected-toward its
objectives of maximum employment

07:18.116 --> 07:19.676 align:middle
and 2 percent inflation.

07:20.296 --> 07:24.296 align:middle
This broad assessment
will not hinge on any one

07:24.296 --> 07:28.406 align:middle
or two indicators, but will
take into account a wide range

07:28.406 --> 07:31.316 align:middle
of information, including
measures

07:31.316 --> 07:33.016 align:middle
of labor market conditions,

07:33.466 --> 07:35.656 align:middle
indicators of inflation
pressures

07:35.656 --> 07:38.356 align:middle
and inflation expectations,
and readings

07:38.356 --> 07:39.936 align:middle
on financial developments.

07:41.116 --> 07:43.946 align:middle
Based on its current
assessment of these factors,

07:44.276 --> 07:48.176 align:middle
the Committee anticipates that
it likely will be appropriate

07:48.656 --> 07:51.506 align:middle
to maintain the current
target range

07:51.506 --> 07:54.986 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,
for a considerable time

07:54.986 --> 07:57.396 align:middle
after the asset purchase
program ends,

07:57.956 --> 08:01.156 align:middle
especially if projected
inflation continues

08:01.156 --> 08:04.856 align:middle
to run below the Committee's
2 percent longer-run goal,

08:05.236 --> 08:10.006 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain

08:10.006 --> 08:10.746 align:middle
well anchored.

08:11.586 --> 08:15.636 align:middle
Further, once we begin to
remove policy accommodation,

08:16.146 --> 08:18.946 align:middle
it's the Committee's
current assessment that,

08:18.946 --> 08:20.456 align:middle
even after employment

08:20.456 --> 08:23.836 align:middle
and inflation are near
mandate-consistent levels,

08:24.476 --> 08:27.136 align:middle
economic conditions
may, for some time,

08:27.626 --> 08:31.716 align:middle
warrant keeping the target
federal funds rate below levels

08:31.716 --> 08:34.346 align:middle
the Committee views as
normal in the longer run.

08:35.596 --> 08:39.756 align:middle
This guidance is consistent with
the paths for appropriate policy

08:40.166 --> 08:43.116 align:middle
as reported in the
participants' projections,

08:43.646 --> 08:45.386 align:middle
which show the federal
funds rate

08:45.386 --> 08:49.766 align:middle
for most participants remaining
well below longer-run normal

08:49.766 --> 08:52.426 align:middle
values at the end of 2016.

08:53.516 --> 08:57.656 align:middle
Although FOMC participants
provide a number of explanations

08:57.886 --> 09:01.406 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
target remaining below its

09:01.436 --> 09:06.096 align:middle
longer-run normal level, many
cite the residual effects

09:06.096 --> 09:07.656 align:middle
of the financial crisis.

09:08.266 --> 09:11.026 align:middle
These include restrained
household spending,

09:11.606 --> 09:15.916 align:middle
reduced credit availability,
and diminished expectations

09:16.206 --> 09:19.426 align:middle
for future growth in
output and incomes,

09:19.786 --> 09:23.446 align:middle
consistent with the view that
the potential growth rate

09:23.446 --> 09:26.716 align:middle
of the economy may be
lower for some time.

09:27.966 --> 09:31.426 align:middle
Let me reiterate, however, that
the Committee's expectation

09:31.426 --> 09:36.086 align:middle
for the path of the federal
funds rate target is contingent

09:36.086 --> 09:37.506 align:middle
on the economic outlook.

09:38.816 --> 09:42.676 align:middle
If the economy proves to be
stronger than anticipated

09:42.676 --> 09:46.816 align:middle
by the Committee, resulting
in a more rapid convergence

09:46.816 --> 09:51.076 align:middle
of employment and inflation
to the FOMC's objectives,

09:52.016 --> 09:55.366 align:middle
then increases in the federal
funds rate target are likely

09:55.366 --> 10:00.036 align:middle
to occur sooner and to be more
rapid than currently envisaged.

10:01.116 --> 10:04.286 align:middle
Conversely, if economic
performance disappoints,

10:04.916 --> 10:08.286 align:middle
resulting in larger and
more persistent deviations

10:08.286 --> 10:11.526 align:middle
from the Committee's
objectives, then increases

10:11.526 --> 10:15.646 align:middle
in the federal funds rate target
are likely to take place later

10:15.976 --> 10:17.406 align:middle
and to be more gradual.

10:18.646 --> 10:22.096 align:middle
Before taking your questions,
I'd like to provide an update

10:22.096 --> 10:26.036 align:middle
on the Committee's ongoing
discussions on the mechanics

10:26.036 --> 10:30.166 align:middle
of normalizing the stance and
conduct of monetary policy.

10:31.136 --> 10:35.066 align:middle
To be clear, these discussions
are in no way intended

10:35.106 --> 10:39.036 align:middle
to signal any imminent change in
the stance of monetary policy.

10:39.786 --> 10:42.566 align:middle
Rather, they represent
prudent planning on the part

10:42.566 --> 10:45.986 align:middle
of the Committee and reflect
the Committee's intention

10:46.356 --> 10:48.016 align:middle
to communicate its plans

10:48.016 --> 10:50.946 align:middle
to the public well
before the first steps

10:50.946 --> 10:53.526 align:middle
in normalizing policy
become appropriate.

10:54.726 --> 10:58.076 align:middle
The Committee is confident
that it has the tools it needs

10:58.076 --> 10:59.906 align:middle
to raise short-term
interest rates

11:00.376 --> 11:04.926 align:middle
when it becomes appropriate to
do so and to control the level

11:04.926 --> 11:07.286 align:middle
of short-term interest
rates thereafter,

11:08.096 --> 11:10.396 align:middle
even though the Federal
Reserve will continue

11:10.426 --> 11:13.386 align:middle
to have a very large
balance sheet for some time.

11:14.606 --> 11:17.376 align:middle
The Committee's recent
discussions have centered

11:17.376 --> 11:20.316 align:middle
on the appropriate mix of tools

11:20.316 --> 11:23.216 align:middle
to employ during the
normalization process

11:23.866 --> 11:27.826 align:middle
and the associated implications
for the degree of control

11:28.246 --> 11:31.586 align:middle
over short-term interest
rates, the functioning

11:31.586 --> 11:35.146 align:middle
of the federal funds
market, and the extent

11:35.146 --> 11:38.096 align:middle
to which the Federal
Reserve transacts

11:38.096 --> 11:41.436 align:middle
with financial institutions
outside the banking sector.

11:42.486 --> 11:44.726 align:middle
The Committee is
constructively working

11:44.796 --> 11:47.816 align:middle
through the many issues
related to normalization

11:48.306 --> 11:50.326 align:middle
and will continue
its discussions

11:50.326 --> 11:53.376 align:middle
in upcoming meetings,
with the expectation

11:53.376 --> 11:56.846 align:middle
of providing additional
details later this year.

11:57.876 --> 11:58.436 align:middle
Thank you.

11:58.706 --> 12:05.966 align:middle
I'll be happy to
take your questions.

12:07.256 --> 12:07.836 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:07.836 --> 12:10.136 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

12:10.766 --> 12:14.216 align:middle
There is every reason
to expect, Madam Chair,

12:14.286 --> 12:17.756 align:middle
that the PCE inflation rate,
which is followed by the Fed,

12:18.266 --> 12:23.766 align:middle
looks likely to exceed your 2016
consensus forecast next week.

12:24.296 --> 12:27.166 align:middle
Does this suggest that the
Federal Reserve is behind the

12:27.166 --> 12:28.516 align:middle
curve on inflation?

12:29.006 --> 12:33.146 align:middle
And what tolerance is
there for higher inflation

12:33.146 --> 12:34.226 align:middle
at the Federal Reserve?

12:34.526 --> 12:36.576 align:middle
And if it's above
the 2 percent target,

12:37.056 --> 12:39.136 align:middle
then how is that
not kind of blowing

12:39.136 --> 12:41.906 align:middle
through a target the same
way you blew through the 6

12:41.906 --> 12:43.366 align:middle
and a half percent
unemployment target

12:43.366 --> 12:45.476 align:middle
in that they become
these soft targets?

12:45.476 --> 12:47.066 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

12:47.066 --> 12:50.096 align:middle
Well, thanks for the question.

12:51.836 --> 12:58.496 align:middle
So I think recent
readings on, for example,

12:58.696 --> 13:04.256 align:middle
the CPI index have been
a bit on the high side,

13:04.256 --> 13:08.856 align:middle
but I think it's-the data
that we're seeing is noisy.

13:09.476 --> 13:12.846 align:middle
I think it's important to
remember that, broadly speaking,

13:12.846 --> 13:15.646 align:middle
inflation is evolving in line

13:15.646 --> 13:17.666 align:middle
with the Committee's
expectations.

13:18.396 --> 13:23.266 align:middle
The Committee, it has
expected a gradual return

13:23.266 --> 13:26.686 align:middle
in inflation toward its
2 percent objective.

13:27.216 --> 13:30.606 align:middle
And I think the recent
evidence we have seen,

13:30.856 --> 13:32.996 align:middle
abstracting from the noise,

13:32.996 --> 13:36.886 align:middle
suggests that we are
moving back gradually

13:36.886 --> 13:39.886 align:middle
over time toward our
2 percent objective,

13:40.186 --> 13:42.506 align:middle
and I see things roughly in line

13:42.506 --> 13:45.896 align:middle
with where we expected
inflation to be.

13:46.116 --> 13:48.916 align:middle
I think if you look
at the SEP projections

13:49.126 --> 13:53.516 align:middle
that were submitted this time,
you see very little change

13:53.656 --> 13:56.336 align:middle
in inflation projections
of the Committee.

13:57.126 --> 13:59.036 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:59.036 --> 14:02.926 align:middle
What about the tolerance
for higher inflation?

14:02.926 --> 14:03.436 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

14:03.436 --> 14:05.656 align:middle
Well, the Committee
has emphasized

14:06.056 --> 14:09.146 align:middle
that we have the 2
percent objective for,

14:09.146 --> 14:13.586 align:middle
as a longer-term
matter, for PCE inflation

14:13.916 --> 14:20.096 align:middle
and we would not willingly
see a prolonged period

14:20.096 --> 14:25.136 align:middle
in which inflation persistently
runs below our objective

14:25.366 --> 14:28.616 align:middle
or above our objective,
and that remains true.

14:29.106 --> 14:32.036 align:middle
So that hasn't changed
at all in terms

14:32.036 --> 14:33.986 align:middle
of the Committee's tolerance

14:33.986 --> 14:37.236 align:middle
for permanent deviations
from our objective.

14:37.576 --> 14:41.696 align:middle
And we continue to see
the data coming in,

14:41.896 --> 14:47.576 align:middle
abstracting from the noise, in
line with what we had expected,

14:48.476 --> 14:51.416 align:middle
and continue to see
a gradual pickup

14:51.496 --> 14:54.386 align:middle
over the next several years
toward our 2 percent objective.

14:54.836 --> 14:56.326 align:middle
ROBIN HARDING.

14:56.776 --> 15:00.046 align:middle
Robin Harding from
the Financial Times.

15:00.046 --> 15:03.046 align:middle
Madam Chair, could you comment
a little bit more on the decline

15:03.046 --> 15:05.876 align:middle
in the long-run interest
rate projection?

15:06.246 --> 15:08.986 align:middle
Is that more to do with
temporary headwinds

15:08.986 --> 15:11.236 align:middle
from the recovery or is it
something more permanent

15:11.236 --> 15:12.136 align:middle
about the economy?

15:12.566 --> 15:15.676 align:middle
And is this it, this
decline to 3.75 percent?

15:15.676 --> 15:16.956 align:middle
Or do you think there's
potential

15:16.956 --> 15:18.996 align:middle
for this rate to go lower yet?

15:19.266 --> 15:19.556 align:middle
Thank you.

15:19.716 --> 15:20.356 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

15:20.356 --> 15:24.436 align:middle
Well, you do see a very
slight decline this time

15:24.696 --> 15:31.026 align:middle
in the Committee's
longer-term normal rate

15:31.026 --> 15:32.656 align:middle
of interest projections.

15:33.216 --> 15:37.276 align:middle
You know, I would caution you,
however, we've had turnover

15:37.276 --> 15:41.946 align:middle
in the Committee-two new
participants who joined

15:41.946 --> 15:46.086 align:middle
and are submitting projections
and two who departed-and

15:47.296 --> 15:52.996 align:middle
that can create changes in
the projections, small changes

15:52.996 --> 15:55.436 align:middle
that are difficult to interpret.

15:55.476 --> 15:58.886 align:middle
But I think it's fair to say
there has been a slight decline.

15:59.126 --> 16:02.106 align:middle
And I think, you know,
the most likely reason

16:02.726 --> 16:07.146 align:middle
for that is there has been some
slight decline, as I mentioned

16:07.146 --> 16:11.376 align:middle
in my opening statement,
of projections pertaining

16:11.656 --> 16:16.076 align:middle
to longer-term growth,
and typically estimates

16:16.076 --> 16:19.586 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal federal funds rate

16:19.876 --> 16:23.106 align:middle
or short-term interest
rates would move in line

16:23.106 --> 16:24.446 align:middle
with growth projections.

16:32.256 --> 16:34.636 align:middle
YLAN MUI. Hi, Ylan from
the Washington Post.

16:34.636 --> 16:36.536 align:middle
My question is sort of
the flip side of Steve's,

16:36.536 --> 16:38.526 align:middle
and it's about your
outlook for unemployment.

16:39.006 --> 16:40.356 align:middle
Your predecessor has said

16:40.356 --> 16:43.776 align:middle
that the Fed has been
consistently too pessimistic

16:43.776 --> 16:46.496 align:middle
about the level of the
unemployment rate, and today,

16:46.496 --> 16:47.856 align:middle
you guys lowered
your outlook again.

16:47.856 --> 16:50.036 align:middle
Can you tell me a
little bit more

16:50.036 --> 16:53.406 align:middle
about how you see the
unemployment rate evolving

16:53.406 --> 16:55.286 align:middle
to meet your forecast?

16:55.476 --> 16:59.126 align:middle
Why you believe the rate of
decline will start to level off?

16:59.196 --> 17:01.146 align:middle
And what an unexpected
drop might mean

17:01.266 --> 17:02.336 align:middle
for the first rate hike.

17:02.336 --> 17:02.856 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

17:03.506 --> 17:07.766 align:middle
So, it's true that unemployment
has declined by more

17:07.766 --> 17:10.106 align:middle
than the Committee expected

17:10.106 --> 17:15.696 align:middle
and you do see a small
downward revision

17:15.696 --> 17:18.886 align:middle
in the Committee's projections,
at least the central tendency

17:18.886 --> 17:20.826 align:middle
for the unemployment rate.

17:20.826 --> 17:25.006 align:middle
Now, first of all, I mean,
the labor market, I think,

17:25.006 --> 17:27.476 align:middle
has continued to
broadly improve.

17:27.886 --> 17:32.686 align:middle
We have seen continued
job growth at a pace

17:32.686 --> 17:34.376 align:middle
that is certainly sufficient

17:34.706 --> 17:38.706 align:middle
to be diminishing labor
market slack over time.

17:39.076 --> 17:42.306 align:middle
Over the last three
months, for example,

17:43.336 --> 17:46.676 align:middle
employment-payroll
employment-has been rising

17:46.676 --> 17:51.146 align:middle
around 230,000 jobs per
month and we're running close

17:51.146 --> 17:54.026 align:middle
to 200,000 over the last year.

17:54.336 --> 17:57.776 align:middle
So, it's in no way
surprising to see a decline

17:57.776 --> 17:59.116 align:middle
in the unemployment rate.

17:59.676 --> 18:05.266 align:middle
That said, many of my colleagues
and I would see a portion

18:05.266 --> 18:08.176 align:middle
of the decline in
the unemployment rate

18:08.176 --> 18:14.256 align:middle
as perhaps not representing
a diminution

18:14.256 --> 18:16.106 align:middle
of slack in the labor market.

18:16.356 --> 18:19.896 align:middle
We have seen labor force
participation rate decline.

18:20.286 --> 18:27.126 align:middle
And while I think most of us
would agree that there has been

18:27.126 --> 18:30.706 align:middle
and will continue to
be secular decline

18:30.706 --> 18:35.346 align:middle
in the labor force participation
rate for demographic reasons,

18:35.756 --> 18:38.866 align:middle
I think a portion of
the decline we've seen

18:38.866 --> 18:43.616 align:middle
in the unemployment rate
probably reflects a kind

18:43.616 --> 18:49.336 align:middle
of shadow unemployment or
discouragement, a cyclical part

18:49.526 --> 18:52.006 align:middle
of labor force participation.

18:52.346 --> 18:57.396 align:middle
Now, if that's correct, we may
see that as the economy picks

18:57.396 --> 19:01.376 align:middle
up steam and we see further
recovery in the labor market,

19:01.776 --> 19:05.796 align:middle
that those, let's call
them discouraged workers,

19:06.606 --> 19:11.696 align:middle
will return either to
unemployment or to employment.

19:12.076 --> 19:17.656 align:middle
And as labor force participation
begins to stabilize,

19:17.856 --> 19:20.646 align:middle
the unemployment rate will
come down less quickly.

19:20.946 --> 19:22.816 align:middle
And I think for a
number of people,

19:22.816 --> 19:25.706 align:middle
that's a component
of the forecast.

19:26.026 --> 19:30.736 align:middle
You asked about implications
for the path of policy

19:30.736 --> 19:34.756 align:middle
and I would just say, the
guidance that we've given,

19:34.836 --> 19:38.416 align:middle
our forward guidance,
states that the timing

19:38.416 --> 19:43.556 align:middle
of liftoff will depend
on actual progress we see

19:43.856 --> 19:47.876 align:middle
and the progress we expect
to see going forward in terms

19:47.876 --> 19:52.056 align:middle
of achieving both of our goals,
namely maximum employment

19:52.316 --> 19:55.176 align:middle
and our 2 percent
inflation objective.

19:55.336 --> 20:00.076 align:middle
So, we're not going to look
at any single indicator

20:00.136 --> 20:03.126 align:middle
like the unemployment rate
to assess how we're doing

20:03.446 --> 20:06.456 align:middle
on meeting our employment
goal; we will look

20:06.456 --> 20:08.296 align:middle
at a broad range of indicators.

20:08.626 --> 20:11.016 align:middle
That said, as I tried
to emphasize

20:11.136 --> 20:13.786 align:middle
in my opening statement,
there is uncertainty

20:13.786 --> 20:15.236 align:middle
about monetary policy.

20:15.576 --> 20:19.916 align:middle
The appropriate path of
policy, the timing in pace

20:19.916 --> 20:23.456 align:middle
of interest rate
increases, ought to

20:23.456 --> 20:29.106 align:middle
and I believe will respond to
unfolding economic developments.

20:29.106 --> 20:32.806 align:middle
If those were to prove faster
than the Committee expects,

20:32.806 --> 20:37.216 align:middle
it would be logical to
expect a more rapid increase

20:37.216 --> 20:38.426 align:middle
in the fed funds rate.

20:38.546 --> 20:40.626 align:middle
But the opposite
also holds true.

20:41.216 --> 20:46.006 align:middle
If we don't see the
improvement that's projected

20:46.006 --> 20:50.956 align:middle
in the baseline outlook, that
the opposite would be true

20:51.346 --> 20:53.786 align:middle
and the pace of the
timing and pace

20:54.306 --> 20:58.756 align:middle
of interest rate increases
would be later and more gradual.

21:03.476 --> 21:04.546 align:middle
JON HILSENRATH.

21:04.546 --> 21:06.806 align:middle
Jon Hilsenrath from the
Wall Street Journal.

21:06.806 --> 21:08.416 align:middle
Chair Yellen, some Fed officials

21:08.416 --> 21:10.556 align:middle
and some market commentators
have noted

21:10.686 --> 21:14.006 align:middle
that market conditions recently
have looked a little bit

21:14.006 --> 21:16.916 align:middle
like they did last spring
before a period of turbulence.

21:17.316 --> 21:20.076 align:middle
Volatility is very low in
stock and bond markets.

21:21.016 --> 21:24.536 align:middle
Risk premiums are very
low, and in particular,

21:24.536 --> 21:28.366 align:middle
the market expectations
for interest rates,

21:28.366 --> 21:31.826 align:middle
for short-term interest rates
look below even the Fed's own

21:32.066 --> 21:33.996 align:middle
projections as laid
out in your dot plot.

21:33.996 --> 21:36.216 align:middle
I wanted to ask two
questions related to that.

21:36.216 --> 21:41.366 align:middle
One, what is your read on
these-on market activity,

21:41.366 --> 21:43.756 align:middle
and are you at all
concerned about a sense

21:43.756 --> 21:45.226 align:middle
of complacency in markets?

21:45.546 --> 21:51.056 align:middle
And, two, what is your
view on market expectations

21:51.056 --> 21:54.296 align:middle
for the rate hike cycle
that the Fed has laid

21:54.296 --> 21:55.306 align:middle
out in its dot plot?

21:55.666 --> 21:59.306 align:middle
Or, is the market where you
think the Fed is on that?

21:59.656 --> 21:59.956 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

22:00.556 --> 22:04.906 align:middle
Well, I mean, I'd start
by saying that volatility,

22:04.906 --> 22:11.326 align:middle
both actual and expected,
in markets is at low levels.

22:11.746 --> 22:17.836 align:middle
The FOMC has no target
for what the right level

22:17.836 --> 22:19.776 align:middle
of volatility should be.

22:20.286 --> 22:23.886 align:middle
But to the extent
that low levels

22:23.886 --> 22:30.366 align:middle
of volatility may induce
risk-taking behavior that,

22:30.626 --> 22:36.656 align:middle
for example, entails
excessive buildup in leverage

22:36.956 --> 22:43.476 align:middle
or maturity extension,
things that can pose risks

22:43.516 --> 22:48.056 align:middle
to financial stability
later on, that is a concern

22:48.056 --> 22:49.726 align:middle
to me and to the Committee.

22:50.376 --> 22:53.856 align:middle
I don't know whether a number
of reasons have been cited

22:53.856 --> 22:56.386 align:middle
for what we're seeing
in the marketplace.

22:56.496 --> 22:58.806 align:middle
I don't know if overconfidence

22:59.096 --> 23:03.486 align:middle
or complacency is
one of those reasons.

23:03.746 --> 23:07.026 align:middle
But I guess I would say, it
is important, as I emphasized

23:07.026 --> 23:11.926 align:middle
in my opening statement, for
market participants to recognize

23:12.326 --> 23:15.516 align:middle
that there is uncertainty
about what the path

23:15.516 --> 23:17.926 align:middle
of interest rates-short-term
rates-will be.

23:18.286 --> 23:21.526 align:middle
And that's necessary
because there's uncertainty

23:21.526 --> 23:23.976 align:middle
about what the path of
the economy will be.

23:24.256 --> 23:26.986 align:middle
And I want to emphasize,
as I have,

23:27.016 --> 23:30.736 align:middle
that the FOMC will adjust policy

23:30.736 --> 23:34.596 align:middle
to what it actually sees
unfolding in the economy

23:34.946 --> 23:40.936 align:middle
over time, and that necessarily
gives rise to a certain level

23:40.936 --> 23:43.896 align:middle
of uncertainty about what
the path of rates will be.

23:44.166 --> 23:48.176 align:middle
And it is important for
market participants to factor

23:48.176 --> 23:51.336 align:middle
that into their decisionmaking.

23:51.596 --> 23:57.786 align:middle
You asked me about the
dot plot-our forecast,

23:57.786 --> 24:03.196 align:middle
or our projections, for the
fed funds rate-and you do see a

24:03.196 --> 24:06.346 align:middle
range of disagreement among
the participants there,

24:06.346 --> 24:10.816 align:middle
so by the time you get to 2016,
there is a considerable range

24:11.096 --> 24:12.816 align:middle
of opinion, and I
think, in part,

24:13.156 --> 24:15.236 align:middle
that reflects the uncertainty

24:15.236 --> 24:20.376 align:middle
that I'm talking about-that
participants do see different

24:20.376 --> 24:24.456 align:middle
pace of recovery, different
trajectories for inflation,

24:24.746 --> 24:28.686 align:middle
and it's appropriate for
them to adjust their thinking

24:28.686 --> 24:35.026 align:middle
about what the path of policy
should be to their own view

24:35.306 --> 24:38.686 align:middle
of how the economy
will evolve over time.

24:38.966 --> 24:40.806 align:middle
And around each of those dots,

24:41.036 --> 24:44.986 align:middle
I think every participant
who's filling

24:44.986 --> 24:49.736 align:middle
out that questionnaire has a
considerable band of uncertainty

24:49.996 --> 24:53.416 align:middle
around their own
individual forecast.

24:54.546 --> 24:57.646 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

24:58.386 --> 24:59.866 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum, New York Times.

24:59.866 --> 25:01.846 align:middle
You've spoken about the sense

25:01.846 --> 25:03.926 align:middle
that the recession has
done permanent damage

25:03.926 --> 25:07.526 align:middle
to the economic output and
you've reduced gradually

25:07.526 --> 25:09.946 align:middle
over time your forecast
of long-term growth.

25:10.466 --> 25:12.686 align:middle
I am curious to know to what
extent you think stronger

25:12.686 --> 25:15.636 align:middle
monetary and/or fiscal policy
could reverse those trends.

25:15.636 --> 25:17.516 align:middle
Are we stuck with slower growth?

25:17.646 --> 25:19.836 align:middle
Is there something that
you can do about it?

25:19.996 --> 25:20.686 align:middle
If so, what?

25:20.686 --> 25:21.526 align:middle
If not, why?

25:22.836 --> 25:23.856 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

25:23.896 --> 25:31.396 align:middle
Well, I think part of the reason
that we are seeing slower growth

25:31.396 --> 25:34.456 align:middle
in potential output
may reflect the fact

25:34.456 --> 25:38.996 align:middle
that capital investment has been
very weak during the downturn

25:38.996 --> 25:42.356 align:middle
in the long recovery
that we're experiencing.

25:42.736 --> 25:46.826 align:middle
So, a diminished contribution
from capital formation

25:46.826 --> 25:50.816 align:middle
to growth does make a negative
contribution to growth.

25:50.816 --> 25:55.276 align:middle
And as the economy picks up,
I certainly would hope to see

25:55.276 --> 25:58.516 align:middle
that contribution restored.

26:00.756 --> 26:05.736 align:middle
So, I think that's one of the
factors that's been operative.

26:06.136 --> 26:13.906 align:middle
Of course, we've had unusually
long duration unemployment.

26:15.316 --> 26:19.946 align:middle
A very large fraction of those
unemployed have been unemployed

26:19.946 --> 26:21.446 align:middle
for more than six months.

26:21.956 --> 26:27.556 align:middle
And there is the fear that
those individuals find it harder

26:27.646 --> 26:31.126 align:middle
to gain employment,
that their attachment

26:31.436 --> 26:36.176 align:middle
to the labor force may diminish
over time and the networks

26:36.176 --> 26:39.276 align:middle
of contacts that are-they
have that are helpful

26:39.506 --> 26:43.386 align:middle
in gaining employment can
begin to erode over time.

26:43.786 --> 26:48.026 align:middle
We could see what's known as
hysteresis, where individuals,

26:48.066 --> 26:52.056 align:middle
because they haven't had
jobs for a long time,

26:52.236 --> 26:55.896 align:middle
find themselves permanently
outside the labor force.

26:56.286 --> 26:59.886 align:middle
My hope would be that
as-and my expectation is

27:00.116 --> 27:05.016 align:middle
that as the economy recovers,
we will see some repair of that,

27:05.076 --> 27:09.576 align:middle
that many of those individuals
who were long-term unemployed

27:09.576 --> 27:11.806 align:middle
or those who are
now counted as out

27:11.806 --> 27:15.966 align:middle
of the labor force would take
jobs if the economy is stronger

27:16.206 --> 27:19.866 align:middle
and would be drawn back in
again, but it is conceivable

27:19.946 --> 27:24.556 align:middle
that there is some permanent
damage there to them,

27:24.556 --> 27:28.096 align:middle
to their own well-being,
their family's well-being,

27:28.096 --> 27:30.246 align:middle
and the economy's potential.

27:30.756 --> 27:32.376 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

27:34.256 --> 27:35.486 align:middle
Thank you, Madam Chair.

27:35.586 --> 27:39.256 align:middle
I believe you mentioned in your
opening remarks tighter credit,

27:39.976 --> 27:44.686 align:middle
and I'm wondering what you
think of the possibility

27:44.686 --> 27:48.206 align:middle
that the Federal Reserve itself,
with the regulations it has

27:48.206 --> 27:51.476 align:middle
to impose under Dodd-Frank, is
partly responsible for that.

27:52.196 --> 27:54.596 align:middle
And, second, the current
trend toward litigation.

27:54.596 --> 27:58.666 align:middle
I recently read something where
just the three largest banks

27:58.666 --> 28:02.206 align:middle
in the U.S. have paid $51
billion dollars in fines so far,

28:02.746 --> 28:04.866 align:middle
and obviously, the
number is rising.

28:05.416 --> 28:09.336 align:middle
So, why would anybody loan
to a near-prime borrower?

28:09.416 --> 28:12.226 align:middle
And, in fact, if you look
at Federal Reserve Bank

28:12.226 --> 28:13.946 align:middle
of New York research,
here we are,

28:14.266 --> 28:18.616 align:middle
five years into the
expansion, and people below FICO

28:18.616 --> 28:22.026 align:middle
of 700 are having worse
credit experiences.

28:22.346 --> 28:25.386 align:middle
So it probably isn't
because, you know,

28:25.386 --> 28:26.706 align:middle
unemployment's declining.

28:26.706 --> 28:28.516 align:middle
It's probably because
banks simply don't want

28:28.516 --> 28:29.316 align:middle
to take the risk.

28:29.886 --> 28:31.956 align:middle
So, as the nation's
top bank regulator,

28:31.956 --> 28:33.806 align:middle
what can you do to fix that?

28:34.856 --> 28:35.776 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

28:35.906 --> 28:39.026 align:middle
So, I first would
start by saying

28:39.026 --> 28:42.676 align:middle
that I really think it's
essential in the aftermath

28:42.676 --> 28:47.176 align:middle
of this crisis to strengthen
financial regulation

28:47.486 --> 28:52.826 align:middle
and to make the financial
system more robust

28:52.826 --> 28:55.576 align:middle
and to reduce systemic risk.

28:56.046 --> 29:00.106 align:middle
We can see what the costs
of the financial crisis were

29:00.426 --> 29:02.476 align:middle
and I don't think any

29:02.476 --> 29:05.066 align:middle
of us should want to
see that repeated.

29:05.066 --> 29:10.466 align:middle
So, I think the regulations
that we've put in place,

29:10.726 --> 29:13.756 align:middle
most of which follow
from Dodd-Frank,

29:13.926 --> 29:19.566 align:middle
are highly appropriate to create
a more robust financial system

29:19.566 --> 29:22.086 align:middle
that will be a safer
and sounder one

29:22.376 --> 29:24.526 align:middle
for our economy going forward,

29:24.816 --> 29:27.396 align:middle
and I think we're making
progress on doing that.

29:27.396 --> 29:31.556 align:middle
In putting regulations
into place, we have tried

29:31.556 --> 29:37.026 align:middle
to phase them in, in a way that
gives long enough lead times

29:37.026 --> 29:40.886 align:middle
to make sure that in
strengthening the financial

29:40.886 --> 29:43.216 align:middle
system, we don't
produce a credit crunch.

29:43.486 --> 29:46.436 align:middle
And by and large,
my own assessment is

29:46.436 --> 29:50.556 align:middle
that credit is broadly
available in the economy,

29:50.856 --> 29:56.356 align:middle
but there are some exceptions,
and I would agree with much

29:56.356 --> 29:59.036 align:middle
of what you said when it
comes to mortgage credit.

29:59.556 --> 30:04.436 align:middle
I think banks, at this
point, are reluctant to lend

30:04.436 --> 30:08.006 align:middle
to borrowers with
lower FICO scores.

30:09.046 --> 30:13.526 align:middle
They mention in meetings with
us consistently their concerns

30:13.526 --> 30:19.526 align:middle
about putback risk, and I
think they are-it is difficult

30:19.896 --> 30:23.906 align:middle
for any homeowner who doesn't
have pristine credit these days

30:23.906 --> 30:24.876 align:middle
to get a mortgage.

30:25.176 --> 30:27.076 align:middle
I think that is one
of the factors

30:27.076 --> 30:30.626 align:middle
that is causing the
housing recovery to be slow.

30:30.856 --> 30:32.086 align:middle
It's not the only one,

30:32.086 --> 30:34.586 align:middle
but I would agree
with that assessment.

30:34.836 --> 30:38.466 align:middle
And of course, you know,
there were a lot of practices

30:39.636 --> 30:42.776 align:middle
in connection with mortgage
lending that really needed

30:42.856 --> 30:46.176 align:middle
to be changed, we don't want
to go back to those days,

30:46.516 --> 30:50.336 align:middle
but it is important to
clarify-for us to work

30:50.396 --> 30:54.026 align:middle
to clarify the rules
around mortgage lending

30:54.026 --> 30:58.956 align:middle
to create an environment of
greater certainty for lenders

30:58.956 --> 31:01.486 align:middle
to be willing to
extend mortgage credit.

31:03.416 --> 31:04.136 align:middle
JASON LANGE.

31:04.386 --> 31:08.606 align:middle
Good afternoon, Jason
Lange with Reuters.

31:09.506 --> 31:14.336 align:middle
Chair Yellen, the Fed has
slashed its growth projections

31:14.336 --> 31:17.926 align:middle
for this year, and you've
gone to pains to explain

31:17.926 --> 31:21.006 align:middle
that there is uncertainty in
the path of interest rates

31:21.006 --> 31:24.826 align:middle
in the economy, and yet the Fed
central tendency projections

31:24.826 --> 31:28.196 align:middle
for 2015 and 2016
remain quite strong.

31:28.796 --> 31:33.466 align:middle
Are you confident that the U.S.
economy has entered a period

31:33.466 --> 31:35.936 align:middle
of sustained above-trend
economic growth?

31:36.356 --> 31:36.816 align:middle
Thank you.

31:37.956 --> 31:38.506 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

31:38.506 --> 31:41.866 align:middle
Well, when you say "confident,"
I suppose the answer is "no"

31:41.866 --> 31:43.606 align:middle
because there is uncertainty,

31:43.606 --> 31:50.126 align:middle
but I think there are many good
reasons why we should see a

31:50.186 --> 31:53.956 align:middle
period of sustained
growth in excess

31:53.956 --> 31:55.756 align:middle
of the economy's potential.

31:56.126 --> 31:59.636 align:middle
We have a highly
accommodative monetary policy.

31:59.916 --> 32:02.606 align:middle
We have diminishing fiscal drag.

32:03.046 --> 32:05.786 align:middle
We have easing credit
conditions.

32:06.076 --> 32:12.036 align:middle
We have households who are
becoming more comfortable

32:12.086 --> 32:17.706 align:middle
with their debt levels and
more able to service that debt,

32:17.706 --> 32:20.226 align:middle
an improving job market.

32:20.566 --> 32:24.346 align:middle
We have rising home prices
and rising equity prices

32:24.716 --> 32:29.596 align:middle
and an improving global economy,
at least in my estimation.

32:29.596 --> 32:34.626 align:middle
So I think all of those
things ought to be working

32:34.626 --> 32:37.026 align:middle
to produce above-trend growth

32:37.026 --> 32:40.536 align:middle
and I think that's what's
reflected in the forecast.

32:40.726 --> 32:44.356 align:middle
But, nevertheless, as I said,
of course there is uncertainty

32:44.776 --> 32:46.616 align:middle
around that projection.

32:46.916 --> 32:50.156 align:middle
You know, nevertheless, the
labor market has continued

32:50.156 --> 32:54.936 align:middle
to improve, and over a number
of years in which, admittedly,

32:54.936 --> 32:57.926 align:middle
growth has come in at
a disappointing level,

32:58.146 --> 33:02.566 align:middle
we've still seen the labor
market broadly improve,

33:02.566 --> 33:04.096 align:middle
and I expect that to continue.

33:04.426 --> 33:08.496 align:middle
STEVEN BECKNER.

33:08.656 --> 33:11.036 align:middle
Steve Beckner with
MNI, Madam Chair.

33:11.946 --> 33:14.806 align:middle
You mentioned that there were
discussions of the mechanics

33:14.806 --> 33:16.616 align:middle
of normalization, as you put it.

33:16.856 --> 33:19.826 align:middle
I assume that that
involved some review

33:19.826 --> 33:21.616 align:middle
of the-it was the
third anniversary

33:21.616 --> 33:25.586 align:middle
of the June 2011
exit principles.

33:25.586 --> 33:27.646 align:middle
I wonder if I could
get you to elaborate.

33:27.846 --> 33:31.546 align:middle
In particular, is the
Committee reaching a consensus

33:31.946 --> 33:35.126 align:middle
about the reinvestment
and rollover policies,

33:35.126 --> 33:38.526 align:middle
the timing of discontinuing
those policies?

33:38.786 --> 33:40.986 align:middle
And I'd be interested in
your personal view on that.

33:41.586 --> 33:42.056 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:42.166 --> 33:45.696 align:middle
Well, reinvestment
policy was included

33:45.696 --> 33:50.066 align:middle
in our 2011 exit principles,
and it's one of the things

33:50.266 --> 33:53.016 align:middle
that we are discussing
and reconsidering.

33:53.416 --> 33:57.596 align:middle
We have not yet reached- we've
made quite a lot of progress

33:57.596 --> 34:01.836 align:middle
in our discussion, but we've
not yet reached conclusions

34:01.836 --> 34:05.946 align:middle
about that or other
aspects of our package.

34:06.366 --> 34:11.256 align:middle
There are a couple of things
Chairman Bernanke indicated,

34:11.256 --> 34:15.006 align:middle
in contrast to our
2011 principles,

34:15.416 --> 34:17.686 align:middle
that we would be very unlikely

34:17.686 --> 34:19.766 align:middle
to sell mortgage-backed
securities,

34:20.106 --> 34:23.196 align:middle
and that remains the case.

34:23.506 --> 34:26.576 align:middle
Broadly speaking,
some of the principles

34:26.646 --> 34:31.216 align:middle
that were incorporated in
those-in that 2011 package,

34:31.496 --> 34:35.006 align:middle
the notion that we fully
expect our balance sheet

34:35.066 --> 34:40.636 align:middle
to shrink considerably over time
back toward more normal levels,

34:40.896 --> 34:43.966 align:middle
toward levels that
would be consistent

34:43.966 --> 34:46.856 align:middle
with efficiently
conducting monetary policy,

34:47.156 --> 34:48.876 align:middle
that's still an expectation.

34:49.116 --> 34:50.856 align:middle
I believe it's an expectation

34:50.856 --> 34:55.596 align:middle
that eventually our portfolio
will be-consist largely

34:55.596 --> 34:57.356 align:middle
of Treasuries, eventually,

34:57.736 --> 34:59.996 align:middle
but there are quite
a number of details.

35:01.046 --> 35:04.306 align:middle
We have a number-as you
know, a number of tools

35:04.636 --> 35:08.906 align:middle
that we can deploy as we
move to normalize policy:

35:09.466 --> 35:13.926 align:middle
interest on excess reserves,
our overnight RRP facility,

35:15.146 --> 35:19.466 align:middle
term repurchase agreements
with the markets,

35:19.466 --> 35:25.056 align:middle
our Term Deposit Facility, and
exactly how to deploy that set

35:25.056 --> 35:28.776 align:middle
of tools to meet our objective

35:29.146 --> 35:33.276 align:middle
of raising short-the general
level of short-term rates

35:33.276 --> 35:36.366 align:middle
when the time becomes
appropriate, and how best

35:36.396 --> 35:38.696 align:middle
to communicate to the public

35:38.696 --> 35:42.426 align:middle
and to markets how we
are conducting policy

35:42.626 --> 35:44.236 align:middle
and what our objectives are.

35:44.556 --> 35:46.276 align:middle
Those are things
we're discussing

35:46.536 --> 35:52.296 align:middle
and hope we will be able to come
back with a full description,

35:52.596 --> 35:54.916 align:middle
or let's think of
it as a revised set

35:54.916 --> 36:00.906 align:middle
of exit principles,
later this year.

36:05.196 --> 36:05.676 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

36:05.676 --> 36:07.776 align:middle
Madam Chair, Donna Borak
with American Banker.

36:08.336 --> 36:11.416 align:middle
One of the outstanding
reform issues on the plate

36:11.416 --> 36:13.466 align:middle
of the Fed is to
handle the risk related

36:13.466 --> 36:15.816 align:middle
to short-term wholesale funding.

36:15.816 --> 36:17.966 align:middle
You've been very
supportive of this issue,

36:17.966 --> 36:20.486 align:middle
but we've really heard
little progress so far

36:20.486 --> 36:21.536 align:middle
on where things stand.

36:22.186 --> 36:25.006 align:middle
Can you please explain to
us why it's taken so long

36:25.006 --> 36:26.276 align:middle
to get this proposal out?

36:26.656 --> 36:29.236 align:middle
What are some of the aspects
that the Fed is considering

36:29.276 --> 36:31.776 align:middle
in their approach to how
they roll out this rule,

36:31.956 --> 36:33.276 align:middle
and where we may
be in that process?

36:33.276 --> 36:33.586 align:middle
Thank you.

36:33.586 --> 36:34.006 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:34.276 --> 36:38.126 align:middle
So I'm afraid that I can't
give you a detailed timetable

36:38.476 --> 36:41.596 align:middle
for when we will move
forward with that rule.

36:42.006 --> 36:45.016 align:middle
I've been supportive,
Governor Tarullo

36:45.016 --> 36:49.236 align:middle
and others have been supportive,
of taking some action

36:50.406 --> 36:54.886 align:middle
to diminish the incentives
for heavy reliance

36:54.886 --> 36:56.336 align:middle
on short-term funding.

36:56.656 --> 37:00.956 align:middle
We still see that as one of the
risks to the financial system

37:01.306 --> 37:04.586 align:middle
that wasn't really addressed

37:04.586 --> 37:08.286 align:middle
in the risk-based capital
requirements that we put out

37:08.696 --> 37:12.876 align:middle
or in the liquidity coverage
ratio that's out for proposal.

37:13.286 --> 37:17.346 align:middle
Governor Tarullo has
suggested one approach could be

37:17.616 --> 37:21.696 align:middle
to impose a capital requirement
that's related to reliance

37:21.696 --> 37:25.456 align:middle
on wholesale funding, and
in my own past comments,

37:25.456 --> 37:28.426 align:middle
I've been supportive,
but I'm afraid

37:28.426 --> 37:31.726 align:middle
at this point-and this
remains very much on the table

37:32.056 --> 37:35.836 align:middle
to take some action to address
this-it certainly remains

37:35.836 --> 37:39.066 align:middle
on the table as an
unfinished agenda item,

37:39.316 --> 37:41.506 align:middle
but I don't have a
detailed timetable for you.

37:41.506 --> 37:41.856 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

37:41.906 --> 37:44.106 align:middle
Would it be at some point before
the end of the year, at least?

37:44.106 --> 37:45.146 align:middle
Can you say that much?

37:45.986 --> 37:46.346 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

37:46.346 --> 37:47.826 align:middle
I'm not-I'm just not certain.

37:47.826 --> 37:51.086 align:middle
I'm-I just don't have a detailed
timetable for you, I'm sorry.

37:52.716 --> 37:59.246 align:middle
GREG IP. Madam Chair,
Greg Ip of the Economist.

37:59.746 --> 38:02.246 align:middle
This is partly a follow-up
to Steve Liesman's question.

38:02.246 --> 38:03.236 align:middle
How would the Committee respond

38:03.236 --> 38:06.296 align:middle
if inflation did
temporarily move above target

38:06.396 --> 38:08.596 align:middle
in the near term before you
achieve full employment?

38:08.926 --> 38:11.356 align:middle
Your colleague John Williams
and the IMF have both suggested

38:12.166 --> 38:14.686 align:middle
that the Committee might
consider allowing inflation

38:14.686 --> 38:17.196 align:middle
to temporarily overshoot because
that might achieve a faster,

38:17.666 --> 38:19.146 align:middle
larger improvement
in employment.

38:19.626 --> 38:20.646 align:middle
Second question is:

38:20.646 --> 38:22.706 align:middle
Will financial stability
considerations play a role

38:22.706 --> 38:25.306 align:middle
in when and how fast
the Committee normalizes

38:25.306 --> 38:26.536 align:middle
interest rates?

38:27.156 --> 38:27.476 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:27.476 --> 38:32.926 align:middle
So with respect to the question
of overshooting, let me start

38:32.926 --> 38:37.356 align:middle
by saying that inflation
continues

38:37.356 --> 38:45.486 align:middle
to run well below our objective,
and we're still some ways away

38:45.486 --> 38:47.216 align:middle
from maximum employment.

38:47.716 --> 38:53.176 align:middle
And for the moment, I don't
see any tradeoff whatsoever

38:53.176 --> 38:55.196 align:middle
in achieving our two objectives.

38:55.516 --> 38:58.516 align:middle
They both call for the
same policy-namely,

38:58.516 --> 39:01.956 align:middle
a highly accommodative
monetary policy.

39:02.466 --> 39:07.176 align:middle
So, at best, overshooting
of inflation or the thought

39:07.306 --> 39:11.486 align:middle
that we will reach our inflation
objective before we have

39:11.486 --> 39:16.656 align:middle
attained maximum employment, I
suppose I would see, at most,

39:16.886 --> 39:20.056 align:middle
as a risk that we could face
somewhere down the road.

39:20.856 --> 39:23.786 align:middle
Symmetrically, it's
also conceivably a risk

39:23.826 --> 39:28.986 align:middle
that we would reach our maximum
employment objective before

39:28.986 --> 39:31.936 align:middle
we've actually attained
our inflation objective.

39:32.016 --> 39:34.036 align:middle
So there are different ways

39:34.036 --> 39:39.416 align:middle
in which we could conceivably-or
there could conceivably arise

39:39.416 --> 39:43.236 align:middle
policy conflicts or tradeoffs
somewhere down the road.

39:43.706 --> 39:47.956 align:middle
Now, quite some time
ago, the FOMC adopted,

39:48.266 --> 39:51.206 align:middle
and we reaffirmed
just in January,

39:51.206 --> 39:55.596 align:middle
a statement on our longer-run
goals and policy strategy.

39:55.936 --> 40:00.296 align:middle
And what that statement
said is that, first of all,

40:00.506 --> 40:04.886 align:middle
whenever either inflation
or employment are away

40:04.886 --> 40:08.306 align:middle
from their preferred or
mandate-consistent levels,

40:08.616 --> 40:13.966 align:middle
it will always be the
FOMC's policy to make sure

40:13.966 --> 40:18.366 align:middle
that we get back to those target
levels over the medium term.

40:19.016 --> 40:23.226 align:middle
But a principle that's
embodied in that statement is

40:23.226 --> 40:26.906 align:middle
that the Committee will follow
a so-called balanced approach

40:27.156 --> 40:29.276 align:middle
in deciding on its policies.

40:29.616 --> 40:33.766 align:middle
And, essentially, that means
that when we see some conflict

40:33.826 --> 40:37.366 align:middle
between achieving
the two objectives,

40:37.696 --> 40:41.486 align:middle
that we would consider
in deciding

40:41.486 --> 40:44.366 align:middle
on a policy just how far we are

40:44.446 --> 40:47.966 align:middle
from achieving each
of the objectives.

40:47.966 --> 40:49.406 align:middle
And if the distance

40:49.406 --> 40:53.856 align:middle
from achieving an objective
is particularly large,

40:54.096 --> 40:58.176 align:middle
it would be consistent
with a balanced approach

40:58.176 --> 41:01.056 align:middle
that we would tolerate
some movement

41:01.056 --> 41:03.736 align:middle
in the opposite direction
on the other objective.

41:04.056 --> 41:08.156 align:middle
But balanced approach is the
general policy strategy I think

41:08.156 --> 41:08.746 align:middle
we'd follow.

41:09.166 --> 41:11.906 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

41:13.716 --> 41:16.206 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

41:16.206 --> 41:18.576 align:middle
I'd like to ask you about
your signaling mechanism

41:18.576 --> 41:19.406 align:middle
going forward.

41:19.916 --> 41:23.146 align:middle
At this point you haven't
decided on reinvestments.

41:23.146 --> 41:25.566 align:middle
You've told people, don't pay
any attention to the dot plot,

41:25.836 --> 41:28.616 align:middle
and your two mandates,
inflation and unemployment,

41:28.616 --> 41:30.846 align:middle
are backward-looking
lagging indicators.

41:30.846 --> 41:34.226 align:middle
So, if something should
surprise in the economy

41:34.226 --> 41:36.666 align:middle
with only four SEPs and
press conferences a year,

41:36.856 --> 41:40.396 align:middle
how do you signal to the
markets what the Fed is doing

41:40.586 --> 41:43.076 align:middle
so that you don't run the risk
of an event like last September

41:43.076 --> 41:46.826 align:middle
when people were surprised, or
some sort of credibility problem

41:46.826 --> 41:51.336 align:middle
where people feel you're
falling behind the economy?

41:51.336 --> 41:52.546 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

41:52.546 --> 41:55.556 align:middle
Well, you know, again,
we are very attentive

41:55.556 --> 41:59.726 align:middle
to unfolding economic
developments and understand

42:00.026 --> 42:04.026 align:middle
that there can be surprises and
twists and turns in the road,

42:04.326 --> 42:10.716 align:middle
so that the forecasts that
we've made become no longer

42:10.716 --> 42:12.836 align:middle
appropriate and we
need to respond

42:12.836 --> 42:14.606 align:middle
to unfolding developments.

42:15.036 --> 42:18.766 align:middle
I'm personally committed
to communicating

42:18.766 --> 42:24.226 align:middle
with the public whenever
communication is appropriate.

42:24.546 --> 42:29.726 align:middle
We have four press conferences,
but I would feel it appropriate

42:29.976 --> 42:35.196 align:middle
for me to either have additional
communications meetings

42:35.246 --> 42:40.066 align:middle
with the press or to give
speeches or to, in a variety

42:40.066 --> 42:44.516 align:middle
of opportunities I have, to
make clear what the Committee's

42:44.516 --> 42:47.986 align:middle
thinking is, and my
colleagues as well, I think,

42:48.026 --> 42:50.676 align:middle
would feel it entirely
appropriate

42:50.676 --> 42:53.606 align:middle
to communicate changes-changes
in our views.

42:54.036 --> 42:57.686 align:middle
PEDRO DA COSTA.

42:58.016 --> 43:01.076 align:middle
Hi, I'm Pedro da Costa
from Dow Jones Newswires.

43:01.536 --> 43:02.226 align:middle
Thank you very much.

43:02.226 --> 43:04.996 align:middle
Since we are currently
having a World Cup,

43:04.996 --> 43:08.406 align:middle
I thought it would be valid to
ask a question about the world.

43:08.936 --> 43:11.486 align:middle
And I'm surprised-a little
surprised at the optimism

43:11.486 --> 43:14.326 align:middle
of your forecast
given some, you know,

43:14.326 --> 43:16.546 align:middle
the darkening outlook overseas.

43:16.546 --> 43:21.006 align:middle
You've got conflict in the
Ukraine, escalation of war

43:21.006 --> 43:24.156 align:middle
in Iraq with implications
for oil prices

43:24.156 --> 43:26.956 align:middle
that potentially have
global economic impact.

43:28.086 --> 43:32.416 align:middle
You have-excuse me-a European
recovery that's still fairly

43:32.416 --> 43:34.556 align:middle
weak, and emerging markets
that are slowing down sharply.

43:34.556 --> 43:37.466 align:middle
Do you think the U.S.
can be a lone engine

43:37.506 --> 43:39.686 align:middle
of economic recovery globally?

43:40.006 --> 43:42.486 align:middle
And if I could just follow up
very quickly on Greg's question,

43:43.446 --> 43:45.796 align:middle
because you talked about the
two sides of the mandate,

43:45.796 --> 43:48.046 align:middle
but you didn't quite answer
the financial stability part.

43:48.046 --> 43:51.476 align:middle
Do you think-is financial
stability currently preventing

43:51.476 --> 43:53.236 align:middle
the Fed from being
more accommodative

43:53.236 --> 43:53.996 align:middle
than it would like?

43:53.996 --> 43:56.406 align:middle
And if not, when do you expect
that to happen, if at all?

43:57.386 --> 43:57.706 align:middle
Thank you.

43:58.646 --> 43:59.156 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:59.156 --> 44:02.426 align:middle
So, let me-I'm sorry I
didn't answer the last part

44:02.426 --> 44:04.986 align:middle
of Greg's question and
the last part of yours.

44:04.986 --> 44:05.986 align:middle
Let me start there.

44:06.496 --> 44:09.366 align:middle
With respect to financial
stability,

44:09.826 --> 44:14.016 align:middle
we monitor potential threats
to financial stability very,

44:14.426 --> 44:21.276 align:middle
very carefully, and we have
spoken about some-I've spoken

44:21.276 --> 44:25.166 align:middle
in recent congressional
testimonies and speeches

44:25.436 --> 44:29.496 align:middle
about some threats to
financial stability that are

44:29.496 --> 44:33.096 align:middle
on our radar screen,
that we are monitoring.

44:33.436 --> 44:35.596 align:middle
Trends in leverage lending

44:35.596 --> 44:41.876 align:middle
and the underwriting standards
there, diminished risk spreads

44:42.586 --> 44:46.016 align:middle
in lower-grade corporate
bonds-high-yield bonds-have

44:46.016 --> 44:48.276 align:middle
certainly caught our attention.

44:48.276 --> 44:55.086 align:middle
There is some evidence of
reach-for-yield behavior.

44:55.536 --> 45:01.316 align:middle
That's one of the reasons I
mentioned that this environment

45:01.316 --> 45:06.086 align:middle
of low volatility is very
much on my radar screen

45:06.086 --> 45:11.176 align:middle
and would be a concern to me
if it prompted an increase

45:11.176 --> 45:14.476 align:middle
in leverage or other kinds
of risk-taking behavior

45:14.836 --> 45:19.756 align:middle
that could unwind in a sharp
way and provoke a sharp,

45:19.756 --> 45:21.936 align:middle
for example, jump
in interest rates.

45:22.246 --> 45:25.826 align:middle
And we've seen what effect that
can have on the global economy,

45:26.066 --> 45:29.446 align:middle
and I think it's something
that it's important to avoid.

45:29.996 --> 45:33.296 align:middle
But broadly speaking,
if the question is,

45:33.336 --> 45:37.246 align:middle
to what extent is monetary
policy, at this time,

45:37.486 --> 45:40.396 align:middle
being driven by financial
stability concerns,

45:40.776 --> 45:43.786 align:middle
I would say that-well, I
would never take off the table

45:44.126 --> 45:48.176 align:middle
that monetary policy
should-could,

45:48.176 --> 45:50.636 align:middle
in some circumstances, respond.

45:50.866 --> 45:53.866 align:middle
I don't see them
shaping monetary policy

45:54.196 --> 45:56.076 align:middle
in an important way right now.

45:56.596 --> 46:01.626 align:middle
I don't see a broad-based
increase in leverage,

46:02.586 --> 46:07.266 align:middle
rapid increase in credit growth
or maturity transformation,

46:07.676 --> 46:12.776 align:middle
the kinds of broad trends
that would suggest to me

46:12.776 --> 46:16.656 align:middle
that the level of financial
stability risks has risen

46:16.656 --> 46:18.156 align:middle
above a moderate level.

46:18.506 --> 46:24.066 align:middle
And we are using supervisory
tools and regulations both

46:24.066 --> 46:27.166 align:middle
to make the financial
system more robust

46:27.166 --> 46:30.066 align:middle
and to pay particular
attention to areas

46:30.066 --> 46:33.556 align:middle
where we've spotted concerns,
like leverage lending,

46:33.556 --> 46:36.656 align:middle
which is very much a
focus of our supervision.

46:37.176 --> 46:37.666 align:middle
Now, let's see.

46:37.976 --> 46:41.366 align:middle
There was a first part to your
question, and the first part was

46:41.366 --> 46:46.336 align:middle
about global risks, and
we always pay attention

46:46.336 --> 46:51.636 align:middle
to global risks and the likely
evolution of the global economy.

46:52.436 --> 46:56.766 align:middle
You expressed a lot of pessimism
about emerging markets,

46:56.766 --> 46:58.656 align:middle
and I see it more likely

46:58.656 --> 47:01.726 align:middle
that we'll see moderate
growth and a pickup there.

47:02.196 --> 47:09.916 align:middle
Of course, there are
geopolitical risks-the Middle

47:09.916 --> 47:12.476 align:middle
East, developments
in Iraq, of course.

47:12.786 --> 47:18.246 align:middle
They're not only a humanitarian
concern; they are a concern

47:18.246 --> 47:22.266 align:middle
with respect, potentially, to
energy supplies and prices,

47:22.266 --> 47:26.276 align:middle
and so I would certainly
list that as something

47:26.326 --> 47:29.686 align:middle
in the category of
risks to the outlook.

47:30.156 --> 47:35.586 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

47:35.666 --> 47:36.966 align:middle
Peter Barnes with
Fox Business, ma'am.

47:37.226 --> 47:41.326 align:middle
Can I-just to follow up a little
bit on what Pedro asked about.

47:41.326 --> 47:43.786 align:middle
Specifically, what
about equity markets?

47:43.786 --> 47:48.646 align:middle
I mean, right now, today, the
S&amp;P 500 is on track to close

47:49.086 --> 47:51.866 align:middle
at a-another record high.

47:52.416 --> 47:55.616 align:middle
You have said that you have not
seen any evidence of bubbles

47:55.616 --> 47:57.736 align:middle
in equity markets, and
that they have been trading

47:58.116 --> 47:59.486 align:middle
within historic norms.

47:59.486 --> 48:01.216 align:middle
Is that still the case today?

48:01.496 --> 48:01.806 align:middle
Thank you.

48:02.216 --> 48:02.706 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:02.706 --> 48:06.256 align:middle
So I don't have a sense-the
Committee doesn't try

48:06.256 --> 48:11.106 align:middle
to gauge what is the right
level of equity prices.

48:11.456 --> 48:16.286 align:middle
But we do certainly monitor
a number of different metrics

48:16.666 --> 48:21.066 align:middle
that give us a feeling for
where valuations are relative

48:21.106 --> 48:24.516 align:middle
to things like earnings
or dividends,

48:24.566 --> 48:28.896 align:middle
and look at where these
metrics stand in comparison

48:28.896 --> 48:32.396 align:middle
with previous history to
get a sense of whether

48:32.396 --> 48:36.696 align:middle
or not we're moving to valuation
levels that are outside

48:36.696 --> 48:39.876 align:middle
of historical norms, and
I still don't see that.

48:39.876 --> 48:43.206 align:middle
I still don't see that
for equity prices broadly.

48:43.596 --> 48:50.236 align:middle
ANNALYN KURTZ.

48:50.426 --> 48:52.036 align:middle
Annalyn Kurtz with CNNMoney.

48:52.036 --> 48:53.156 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Yellen.

48:53.586 --> 48:56.676 align:middle
I'm wondering: What's the
Fed's general expectation

48:56.676 --> 48:58.966 align:middle
for wage growth this
year and next year?

48:59.366 --> 49:02.216 align:middle
And if inflation
outpaces wage growth,

49:02.296 --> 49:04.886 align:middle
does that scenario
make you more hesitant

49:04.886 --> 49:06.856 align:middle
to raise the federal
funds rate next year?

49:07.216 --> 49:10.806 align:middle
Or if, conversely, wages
rise just enough to keep

49:10.806 --> 49:13.376 align:middle
up with inflation, they're
moving in lockstep, let's say,

49:13.376 --> 49:16.056 align:middle
is that enough to satisfy
what you're looking

49:16.056 --> 49:17.196 align:middle
for in the job market?

49:17.196 --> 49:17.976 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:18.856 --> 49:19.996 align:middle
Well, thanks.

49:20.036 --> 49:21.196 align:middle
That's a great question.

49:23.126 --> 49:27.666 align:middle
You know, I see compensation
growth, broadly speaking,

49:27.926 --> 49:30.636 align:middle
as having been very
well contained.

49:31.006 --> 49:35.836 align:middle
By most measures,
compensation growth is running

49:35.836 --> 49:37.046 align:middle
around 2 percent.

49:37.516 --> 49:42.576 align:middle
So that's real wage growth

49:42.576 --> 49:46.336 align:middle
or real compensation growth
that's essentially flat rather

49:46.336 --> 49:49.746 align:middle
than rising, and real wage
growth really has not been

49:49.746 --> 49:52.896 align:middle
rising in line with
productivity.

49:53.656 --> 49:59.206 align:middle
My own expectation is that
as the labor market begins

49:59.206 --> 50:04.716 align:middle
to tighten, we will see wage
growth pick up some to the point

50:04.716 --> 50:08.006 align:middle
where real wage growth,
where compensation

50:08.006 --> 50:13.966 align:middle
or nominal wages are rising
more rapidly than inflation,

50:14.266 --> 50:19.976 align:middle
so households are getting a real
increase in their take-home pay.

50:20.416 --> 50:25.116 align:middle
And within limits-well,
that might be signs

50:25.116 --> 50:27.576 align:middle
of a tighter labor market.

50:27.796 --> 50:33.136 align:middle
Within limits, it's not a threat
to inflation because consistent

50:33.136 --> 50:36.116 align:middle
with the level of
inflation we have

50:36.116 --> 50:38.616 align:middle
for our 2 percent
inflation objective,

50:39.006 --> 50:41.766 align:middle
we could see wages growing

50:41.766 --> 50:46.216 align:middle
at a more rapid rate and-a
somewhat more rapid rate-and,

50:46.346 --> 50:49.726 align:middle
indeed, that would be part of
my forecast of what we would see

50:49.936 --> 50:51.796 align:middle
as the labor market picks up.

50:52.396 --> 50:56.106 align:middle
If we were to fail to see
that, frankly, I would worry

50:56.136 --> 50:58.776 align:middle
about downside risk
to consumer spending.

51:00.176 --> 51:03.836 align:middle
So I think part of my
confidence in the fact

51:04.016 --> 51:07.686 align:middle
that we'll see a pickup in
growth relates to the fact

51:07.686 --> 51:11.456 align:middle
that I think consumer
spending will continue

51:11.456 --> 51:13.186 align:middle
to grow at a healthy rate.

51:13.486 --> 51:18.226 align:middle
And, in part, that's premised
on some pickup in the rate

51:18.226 --> 51:20.946 align:middle
of wage growth so that
it's rising greater-more

51:20.946 --> 51:21.746 align:middle
than inflation.

51:22.196 --> 51:24.186 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

51:28.426 --> 51:30.206 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

51:30.596 --> 51:34.406 align:middle
Today's statement repeated a
phrase that's been used-that the

51:34.406 --> 51:37.136 align:middle
Committee has been using,
that there's likely to be a

51:37.136 --> 51:41.066 align:middle
"considerable" period between
the end of the bond purchases

51:41.066 --> 51:43.746 align:middle
and the first hike in
the federal funds rate.

51:44.036 --> 51:46.726 align:middle
In March, you gave us some
guidance to help-trying

51:46.726 --> 51:49.326 align:middle
to help us to understand that
by saying that that-it's hard

51:49.326 --> 51:52.126 align:middle
to define, but it
could mean six months.

51:52.536 --> 51:56.186 align:middle
Is that a time frame
that you feel-still feel

51:56.186 --> 51:56.986 align:middle
comfortable with?

51:57.326 --> 52:00.156 align:middle
And, if you feel like
it needs to be modified,

52:00.556 --> 52:06.756 align:middle
do you-could you give us an
assessment of-the market seemed

52:06.756 --> 52:08.746 align:middle
to expect a rate,
the first rate hike

52:09.106 --> 52:10.676 align:middle
in the second half of next year.

52:10.676 --> 52:11.716 align:middle
Is that a good assessment?

52:11.746 --> 52:12.156 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:12.596 --> 52:15.566 align:middle
So what I want to say,
the guidance that I want

52:15.566 --> 52:20.266 align:middle
to give you, is that there is
no mechanical formula whatsoever

52:20.556 --> 52:24.146 align:middle
for what a "considerable
time" means.

52:24.616 --> 52:29.306 align:middle
The answer as to what
it means is, it depends.

52:29.776 --> 52:33.596 align:middle
It depends on how the
economy progresses.

52:34.106 --> 52:37.866 align:middle
The Committee said very
clearly in their statement

52:38.216 --> 52:42.206 align:middle
that what they would be looking
at and deciding on the timing

52:42.476 --> 52:47.816 align:middle
of interest rate increases would
be the progress we're making

52:47.816 --> 52:51.086 align:middle
in achieving our
objectives, how far we are

52:51.086 --> 52:54.086 align:middle
from achieving our
labor market objective

52:54.376 --> 52:59.886 align:middle
and our inflation objective,
and that we will be assessing

52:59.886 --> 53:02.786 align:middle
that progress, and
that's the key determinant

53:02.986 --> 53:06.186 align:middle
of when interest rate
increases are likely to come.

53:06.646 --> 53:08.906 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

53:08.906 --> 53:09.256 align:middle
Next year?

53:09.256 --> 53:09.596 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

53:09.746 --> 53:11.576 align:middle
There is no mechanical formula.

53:13.686 --> 53:14.526 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

53:18.916 --> 53:21.066 align:middle
Thank you.

53:23.656 --> 53:25.366 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

53:26.076 --> 53:27.676 align:middle
There was a report this week,

53:27.676 --> 53:30.446 align:middle
in a salmon-colored
newspaper I won't mention,

53:31.006 --> 53:34.086 align:middle
that the Fed is thinking
about-and regulators

53:34.086 --> 53:36.766 align:middle
in Washington are
thinking about-an exit fee

53:36.766 --> 53:38.016 align:middle
for bond mutual funds.

53:38.106 --> 53:40.816 align:middle
This has sparked
a lot of comments.

53:40.896 --> 53:43.316 align:middle
Would you care to
comment on this?

53:43.316 --> 53:43.806 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

53:43.906 --> 53:48.116 align:middle
I am not aware of any discussion

53:48.116 --> 53:52.726 align:middle
of that topic inside the Federal
Reserve, and my understanding is

53:52.796 --> 54:03.456 align:middle
that that is a matter that is
under the purview of the SEC.

